{t:"script.reset_config",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-reset-config",b:"3> script. reset_config (_own=None) Needs AutoCAD Deletes this tool's settings file, if there is one, so the defaults apply again. python Copy script.store_data # script. store_data (key, va"},
{t:"script.store_data",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-store-data",b:"3> script. store_data (key, value, per_document=False, _own=None) Needs AutoCAD Saves a JSON-serialisable value under key in this tool's data file, %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\data\\cfg_<bundle key>.jso"},
{t:"script.load_data",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-load-data",b:"3> script. load_data (key, default=None, per_document=False, _own=None) Needs AutoCAD A value saved with store_data , or default when it is not there or the file is missing or corrupt. pytho"},
{t:"script.data_exists",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-data-exists",b:"3> script. data_exists (key, per_document=False, _own=None) Needs AutoCAD True when key is in this tool's data file. python Copy script.get_data_file # script. get_data_file (suffix='json') "},
{t:"script.get_data_file",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-get-data-file",b:"3> script. get_data_file (suffix='json') Needs AutoCAD The path of this tool's shared data file with the given extension, for when you want to write the file yourself (a CSV log, say). The f"},
{t:"script.bind",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-bind",b:"3> script. bind () Needs AutoCAD Pins this command's identity for code that runs after script.py has returned, and returns a BoundScript . Call it at the top of the script, while the identit"},
{t:"script.BoundScript",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-boundscript",b:"3> script. BoundScript (own) BoundScript. get_config (defaults) BoundScript. save_config (values) BoundScript. reset_config () BoundScript. store_data (key, value, per_document=False) BoundS"},
{t:"script.get_logger",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-get-logger",b:"3> script. get_logger () Needs AutoCAD A logger for this script with debug , info , success , warning and error methods (each takes one message) and set_level(level) , where level is 'debug'"},
{t:"script.clipboard_copy",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-clipboard-copy",b:"3> script. clipboard_copy (text) Needs AutoCAD Puts text on the Windows clipboard. python Copy script.clipboard_text # script. clipboard_text () Needs AutoCAD The clipboard's text, or '' whe"},
{t:"script.clipboard_text",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-clipboard-text",b:"3> script. clipboard_text () Needs AutoCAD The clipboard's text, or '' when it holds none or another program has it locked. Never raises. python Copy script.open_url # script. open_url (url)"},
{t:"script.open_url",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-open-url",b:"3> script. open_url (url) Needs AutoCAD Opens a URL in the default browser. python Copy script.show_in_explorer # script. show_in_explorer (path) Needs AutoCAD Opens Explorer with the file s"},
{t:"script.show_in_explorer",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#script-show-in-explorer",b:"3> script. show_in_explorer (path) Needs AutoCAD Opens Explorer with the file selected, or at the folder. python Copy settings: per-tool settings files # One JSON file per tool key under %AP"},
{t:"settings: per-tool settings files",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#settings",b:"2> One JSON file per tool key under %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\settings . Pure standard library with no AutoCAD and no runtime, so it works in any engine, in hooks and panes, and outside AutoCAD. scri"},
{t:"settings.load",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#settings-load",b:"3> settings. load (tool, defaults) Pure The tool's saved values merged over defaults ( settings.merge ). A missing, unreadable or non-object file gives a fresh copy of the defaults; it never"},
{t:"settings.save",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#settings-save",b:"3> settings. save (tool, values) Pure Writes values as the tool's settings file (sorted, indented JSON), creating the folder when needed. The write is atomic: a crash leaves the old file int"},
{t:"settings.merge",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#settings-merge",b:"3> settings. merge (defaults, stored) Pure defaults overlaid with stored : only keys in defaults survive, so stale keys from an older version of a tool never leak back. python Copy settings."},
{t:"settings.path_for",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#settings-path-for",b:"3> settings. path_for (tool) Pure The settings file path for a tool key: %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\settings\\<tool>.json . python Copy xl: Excel workbooks # Reads and writes .xlsx files in pure Python"},
{t:"xl: Excel workbooks",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#xl",b:"2> Reads and writes .xlsx files in pure Python, with no Excel and no third-party package, so it runs the same under IronPython and CPython, inside or outside AutoCAD. It writes a minimal val"},
{t:"xl.write",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#xl-write",b:"3> xl. write (path, rows, headers=None, sheet='Sheet1') Pure Writes rows (lists of str , int , float , bool or None ) to the sheet sheet of the workbook at path , with headers as a bold firs"},
{t:"xl.read",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#xl-read",b:"3> xl. read (path, sheet=None) Pure The rows of the named sheet, or the first sheet when sheet is None . Numbers come back as float , booleans as True or False , everything else as str . A r"},
{t:"xl.sheets",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#xl-sheets",b:"3> xl. sheets (path) Pure The workbook's sheet names, in order. python Copy The private modules # These turn up in tracebacks. Do not import them."},
{t:"The private modules",p:"Python API reference",u:"api-reference.html#private-modules",b:"2> These turn up in tracebacks. Do not import them."},
{t:"Cookbook",p:"Page",u:"cookbook.html",b:"Complete, runnable recipes for the jobs an AutoCAD tool actually has to do, each with its sharp edge named."},
{t:"Selecting and editing entities",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#selecting-and-editing-entities",b:"2> Select everything on chosen layers # A layer picker, then every object on the picked layers becomes the selection, ready for the next command. The one subtlety is the filter: sel.get(laye"},
{t:"Select everything on chosen layers",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#select-everything-on-chosen-layers",b:"3> A layer picker, then every object on the picked layers becomes the selection, ready for the next command. The one subtlety is the filter: sel.get(layer=...) hands the text to AutoCAD's se"},
{t:"Change the colour of the selected objects",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#change-the-colour-of-the-selected-objects",b:"3> Works on the pickfirst set when there is one and asks for a selection when there is not, the behaviour people expect from a native command. Each ents.set call is its own transaction, so a"},
{t:"Find and replace text",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#find-and-replace-text",b:"3> Replaces text in single-line and multiline text, in the selection or else the whole drawing. ents.get gives a TEXT's string and an MTEXT's raw contents, and ents.set(text=...) writes the "},
{t:"Count entities by type",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#count-entities-by-type",b:"3> A quick census of model space, as a table and a bar chart in the output window. ents.type_of needs no transaction, so it is the cheap way to classify thousands of handles. script.py Copy "},
{t:"Select blocks by attribute value",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#select-blocks-by-attribute-value",b:"3> Pick a tag, type part of a value, and every block reference whose attribute contains it is selected. blocks.snapshots() reads every reference in one transaction, so the filtering is plain"},
{t:"Layers",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#layers",b:"2> Rename layers by pattern # Pick the layers with a wildcard, say what to replace in their names, see the whole plan, then rename. Layer 0, Defpoints and xref layers (the ones with a | ) ar"},
{t:"Rename layers by pattern",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#rename-layers-by-pattern",b:"3> Pick the layers with a wildcard, say what to replace in their names, see the whole plan, then rename. Layer 0, Defpoints and xref layers (the ones with a | ) are left out before the plan "},
{t:"Freeze layers by prefix",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#freeze-layers-by-prefix",b:"3> Asks for a prefix, remembers the last one, and freezes every layer that starts with it. The answer goes back into the tool's settings with script.save_config , so the next run offers it a"},
{t:"Create a standard set of layers",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#create-a-standard-set-of-layers",b:"3> Creates whichever layers of an office standard are missing and leaves existing ones alone. The list lives at the top of the script, where the next person can see and change it. script.py "},
{t:"Save and restore a layer state per drawing",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#save-and-restore-a-layer-state-per-drawing",b:"3> A two-button layer state: save every layer's on, frozen and locked flags for this drawing, and put them back later. per_document=True keeps one saved state per drawing, so saving in one d"},
{t:"Report layers in the output window",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#report-layers-in-the-output-window",b:"3> Every layer with its state and how many objects are on it, as a table you can save as HTML. The object count is one filtered select-all per layer, so it reuses the literal helper from the"},
{t:"Blocks and attributes",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#blocks-and-attributes",b:"2> Count blocks by name to Excel # One row per block name with its count, written to a workbook beside the drawing. blocks.schedule_rows does the counting and sorting; xl.write needs no Exce"},
{t:"Count blocks by name to Excel",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#count-blocks-by-name-to-excel",b:"3> One row per block name with its count, written to a workbook beside the drawing. blocks.schedule_rows does the counting and sorting; xl.write needs no Excel and no extra package. script.p"},
{t:"Export the attributes of one block to CSV",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#export-the-attributes-of-one-block-to-csv",b:"3> Pick a block, get a CSV with one row per reference and one column per attribute tag, in the order the block defines them. CSV opens in Excel, imports into anything, and diffs well in vers"},
{t:"Insert a block at picked points",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#insert-a-block-at-picked-points",b:"3> Choose a block, then click as many insertion points as you like; Enter or Esc stops. A banner holds the instruction on screen while the command line asks for each point. script.py Copy Th"},
{t:"Put a block schedule table in the drawing",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#put-a-block-schedule-table-in-the-drawing",b:"3> Counts the drawing's block references and draws the result as an AutoCAD table at a picked point, in the current table style. script.py Copy The table goes into the current space, so run "},
{t:"Renumber room tags in reading order",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#renumber-room-tags-in-reading-order",b:"3> Numbers every TAG-ROOM reference from a starting number, top row first and left to right within a row. All the changes go into the drawing in one blocks.apply_changes call, which reports "},
{t:"Measuring",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#measuring",b:"2> Total length of polylines on a layer # Pick a layer and get the total length of every polyline on it, in the drawing's own units, as a banner across the bottom of the window. ents.lengths"},
{t:"Total length of polylines on a layer",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#total-length-of-polylines-on-a-layer",b:"3> Pick a layer and get the total length of every polyline on it, in the drawing's own units, as a banner across the bottom of the window. ents.lengths measures them all in one transaction. "},
{t:"Length by layer for a selection",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#length-by-layer-for-a-selection",b:"3> Select pipes, ducts or cable runs, and get a takeoff: pieces and total length per layer. ents.length_by_layer does the grouping, so the script is three calls and a table. script.py Copy C"},
{t:"Xrefs, layouts and plotting",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#xrefs-layouts-and-plotting",b:"2> List xrefs with broken paths # Every xref AutoCAD could not find, with its saved path, in the output window. When all of them resolve, a toast says so and no window opens. script.py Copy "},
{t:"List xrefs with broken paths",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#list-xrefs-with-broken-paths",b:"3> Every xref AutoCAD could not find, with its saved path, in the output window. When all of them resolve, a toast says so and no window opens. script.py Copy An unloaded xref is not broken "},
{t:"Repath xrefs after a server move",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#repath-xrefs-after-a-server-move",b:"3> Replace part of every xref path (an old server or drive) with something new. The plan is worked out first by the pure xrefs.plan_repath , shown to the user, and only then applied. Afterwa"},
{t:"Batch plot layouts to PDF",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#batch-plot-layouts-to-pdf",b:"3> Tick the layouts, pick a folder, and each one is plotted to its own PDF named after the drawing and the layout, with a progress window that can be cancelled between sheets. A sheet that f"},
{t:"Drawing health",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#drawing-health",b:"2> Purge, clean and audit in one click # Deletes zero-length lines and polylines, purges everything unused, then queues AUDIT, and reports with the same wording as the shipped Drawing Cleanu"},
{t:"Purge, clean and audit in one click",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#purge-clean-and-audit-in-one-click",b:"3> Deletes zero-length lines and polylines, purges everything unused, then queues AUDIT, and reports with the same wording as the shipped Drawing Cleanup tool by handing the counts to audit."},
{t:"Delete duplicate geometry, with a confirmation",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#delete-duplicate-geometry-with-a-confirmation",b:"3> Finds entities stacked on top of each other (same space, type, layer and geometry), says how many, and deletes every copy but one. Copies on locked layers are left and counted. script.py "},
{t:"Button kinds, hooks and panes",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#button-kinds-hooks-and-panes",b:"2> The next four recipes are one small extension: a toggle that switches a save log on and off, the hook that writes the log, a smartbutton, and a dock pane. Laid out together it looks like "},
{t:"A toggle that remembers its state",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#a-toggle-that-remembers-its-state",b:"3> A *.toggle bundle's on or off state ( script.get_toggle_state ) lasts only for the AutoCAD session and starts off. To remember a choice across restarts, keep the real value in pyacad.sett"},
{t:"A hook that logs every save",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#a-hook-that-logs-every-save",b:"3> A file in the extension's hooks folder named after an event runs every time the event fires, with no click. This one appends a CSV line per save while the toggle above is on. Hooks run ou"},
{t:"A smartbutton that disables itself",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#a-smartbutton-that-disables-itself",b:"3> A *.smartbutton runs its script once more while the ribbon is built, with __selfinit__ set to True and a __button__ to change its own title, tooltip, icon and enabled state. This one appl"},
{t:"A dock pane with a live layer list",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#a-dock-pane-with-a-live-layer-list",b:"3> A *.dockpane is an AutoCAD palette: pane.xaml is its content and script.py runs once against it with the loaded root as __pane__ ( Find(name) , Content , Visible , Title ). The ribbon get"},
{t:"A XAML dialog bundle",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#a-xaml-dialog-bundle",b:"3> When a tool needs three or four answers at once, chained prompts get tedious; a small WPF window is the next step. forms.WPFWindow loads a XAML file from the bundle folder, gives it pyACA"},
{t:"Beyond the ribbon",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#beyond-the-ribbon",b:"2> Call a tool from AutoLISP # Every pushbutton is an AutoCAD command, and AutoLISP can call it as a function: (pyacad \"PYNAME\" args...) runs the script with the arguments in __args__ and re"},
{t:"Call a tool from AutoLISP",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#call-a-tool-from-autolisp",b:"3> Every pushbutton is an AutoCAD command, and AutoLISP can call it as a function: (pyacad \"PYNAME\" args...) runs the script with the arguments in __args__ and returns whatever the script pu"},
{t:"A CPython tool with pandas",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#a-cpython-tool-with-pandas",b:"3> The default engine is IronPython, which runs inside AutoCAD with no install but cannot load compiled packages such as numpy and pandas. A bundle that needs them declares engine: cpython a"},
{t:"A long job with progress and cancel",p:"Cookbook",u:"cookbook.html#a-long-job-with-progress-and-cancel",b:"3> The batch functions take progress and cancelled callbacks and call them every 200 items. Wire them to forms.progress and a long measurement gets a moving bar and a working Cancel button, "},
{t:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",p:"Page",u:"ai-assistant.html",b:"The Ask AI panel, what it sends and what it writes, and how to get the same result from any chatbot you already use."},
{t:"The panel is a beta, and not the only way",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#the-panel-is-a-beta-and-not-the-only-way",b:"2> The panel is new and lightly tested, and says so at the top. Read every script it proposes before you click Create. And you do not need it at all: any assistant that can read a long docum"},
{t:"Setting it up",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#setting-it-up",b:"2> Click AI Settings . The section has five fields: Saved settings apply from your next message; no Reload is needed for them. Where the settings and the key live # Provider, base URL, model"},
{t:"Where the settings and the key live",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#where-the-settings-and-the-key-live",b:"3> Provider, base URL, model and answer length are the \"ai\" section of %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\config.json . Only values that differ from the defaults are written: %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\config.json Copy"},
{t:"Using the panel",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#using-the-panel",b:"2> Click Ask AI . Without a usable key the palette says so and offers Open AI settings . Describe the tool in the box at the bottom and press Send (or Ctrl + Enter ). Tick Attach selection f"},
{t:"Coming back to a tool",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#coming-back-to-a-tool",b:"3> The header's Edit an existing tool list holds every tool in the AI extension. Picking one starts a fresh conversation that is already editing it: its current files ride along with your ne"},
{t:"The fix loop",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#the-fix-loop",b:"3> Every script that fails has its traceback kept for the session, per bundle folder. When the tool the conversation is editing has one, the transcript offers Send last error to AI , which s"},
{t:"What the model is given",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#what-the-model-is-given",b:"2> Every request carries a system prompt in two parts: The output rules. Answer with one bundle; a Title: line; an Icon: line naming one or two letters and a colour; one fenced block per fil"},
{t:"What comes back, and what is written",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#what-comes-back-and-what-is-written",b:"2> The reply is parsed for fenced blocks named bundle.yaml , script.py or config.py , named in the fence's info string or on the line just above it. Any other file name is refused and never "},
{t:"When a reply goes wrong",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#when-a-reply-goes-wrong",b:"2> Request failures are also written to %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\logs\\pyACAD.log as AI request failed . Doing the same with any assistant # In the Ask AI palette, click Copy guide . The clipboard no"},
{t:"Doing the same with any assistant",p:"Writing tools with an AI assistant",u:"ai-assistant.html#doing-the-same-with-any-assistant",b:"2> In the Ask AI palette, click Copy guide . The clipboard now holds the output rules and the authoring guide. (The footer's Docs button opens this page.) Paste it into your chatbot as the f"},
{t:"Troubleshooting",p:"Page",u:"troubleshooting.html",b:"Every way pyACAD and a bundle fail, listed by what you actually see, with the cause, the fix, and the log line that proves it."},
{t:"Where to look first",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#where-to-look-first",b:"2> Both logs are plain text, appended forever, one line per event with a timestamp and [INFO ] or [ERROR] . Search for ERROR first. pyACAD does not load at all # No pyACAD tab, and PYACAD is"},
{t:"pyACAD does not load at all",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#pyacad-does-not-load-at-all",b:"2> No pyACAD tab, and PYACAD is an unknown command. Work down this figure. .tb1-t { font: 12px 'Segoe UI Variable Text','Segoe UI',sans-serif; fill: var(--ink); } .tb1-m { font: 11px 'Segoe "},
{t:"AutoCAD did not load the loader",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#autocad-did-not-load-the-loader",b:"3> Cause. AutoCAD loads pyACAD as an Autodesk application bundle: %APPDATA%\\Autodesk\\ApplicationPlugins\\pyACAD.bundle , whose PackageContents.xml has one block per AutoCAD year, each pointin"},
{t:"The security prompt",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-security-prompt",b:"3> Cause. With SECURELOAD at its default of 1, AutoCAD stops at a Security - Unsigned Executable File prompt before loading an unsigned DLL from a folder it does not trust. If you dismissed "},
{t:"The runtime could not be found",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-runtime-could-not-be-found",b:"3> text Copy Cause. The loader is a small DLL; everything else lives in the runtime folder, %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\<year>\\runtime . The loader looks for it in this order: the PYACAD_RUNTIME enviro"},
{t:"The pyACAD tab is missing",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-pyacad-tab-is-missing",b:"2> A boot stage failed # text Copy Cause. Boot and Reload run their stages (event wiring, shortcuts, startup scripts, dock panes, ribbon markers, commands, ribbon, hooks, context rules, keyb"},
{t:"A boot stage failed",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-boot-stage-failed",b:"3> text Copy Cause. Boot and Reload run their stages (event wiring, shortcuts, startup scripts, dock panes, ribbon markers, commands, ribbon, hooks, context rules, keyboard shortcuts) one by"},
{t:"The tab vanished after a workspace switch",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-tab-vanished-after-a-workspace-switch",b:"3> Cause. Switching workspace, or loading a CUIx, rebuilds AutoCAD's ribbon from the customisation file, which knows nothing of pyACAD's tabs. Fix. Usually none: pyACAD watches for it and pu"},
{t:"The whole tab is missing, not just one button",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-whole-tab-is-missing-not-just-one-button",b:"3> Cause. A panel with no items is not added to its tab, and a tab with no panels is not added to the ribbon. Emptiness propagates upward, so one broken button in a one-button extension remo"},
{t:"A button is missing",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-button-is-missing",b:"2> Look at the toast first After every boot and Reload, pyACAD counts the problems it found in the bundle folders and toasts N bundle problems found , quoting the first one in full. If you s"},
{t:"You did not Reload",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#you-did-not-reload",b:"3> Cause. The ribbon is built from a scan. New folders, renamed folders and new bundle.yaml values are picked up by a rescan, not by the next click. Fix. Click Reload on the pyACAD panel, or"},
{t:"The extension root is not registered",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-extension-root-is-not-registered",b:"3> Cause. pyACAD scans the folders listed under \"extensions\" in %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\config.json , plus two defaults: %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\extensions , and %PROGRAMDATA%\\pyACAD\\extensions if that fo"},
{t:"The machine-wide root was skipped",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-machine-wide-root-was-skipped",b:"3> Cause. Anything in %PROGRAMDATA%\\pyACAD\\extensions runs for every user on the PC, hooks and startup.py included, with no click. So that folder is scanned only when ordinary users cannot w"},
{t:"The .extension folder is nested too deep",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-extension-folder-is-nested-too-deep",b:"3> Cause. .extension folders are found directly under a registered root. The scan does not recurse, so D:\\CadTools\\Team\\Acme.extension under a root of D:\\CadTools is not found. Fix. Move the"},
{t:"A folder suffix is misspelled",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-folder-suffix-is-misspelled",b:"3> Cause. Suffixes are matched case-insensitively but must be the exact word. Count.pushButton works; Count.pushbuttons , Count.push-button and Count.button do not. Log. A folder inside a .p"},
{t:"The bundle is incomplete",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-bundle-is-incomplete",b:"3> It is there, but in the wrong place # Cause. Order comes from the layout: list in the container's own bundle.yaml ( extension.yaml for tabs). A folder the list does not name is not lost: "},
{t:"It is there, but in the wrong place",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#it-is-there-but-in-the-wrong-place",b:"3> Cause. Order comes from the layout: list in the container's own bundle.yaml ( extension.yaml for tabs). A folder the list does not name is not lost: it goes after every listed one, in nam"},
{t:"The same extension is in two roots",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-same-extension-is-in-two-roots",b:"3> Cause. Two copies of one extension on two registered roots, typically a development copy and an installed copy. The copy in the root listed first builds the ribbon. The second copy's butt"},
{t:"The button is greyed out",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-button-is-greyed-out",b:"3> Cause. The bundle has a context: rule that is false right now: selection with nothing selected, paperspace on the Model tab. An unknown condition name is legal but always false, so a typo"},
{t:"Commands",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#commands",b:"2> A command was not registered # text Copy Cause. Two bundles derive the same name (same title in two extensions), a title derives one of the loader's names ( PYACAD , PYACADRELOAD , PYACAD"},
{t:"A command was not registered",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-command-was-not-registered",b:"3> text Copy Cause. Two bundles derive the same name (same title in two extensions), a title derives one of the loader's names ( PYACAD , PYACADRELOAD , PYACADCONSOLE , PYACADRUN , PYACADSET"},
{t:"Typing the command says Unknown command",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#typing-the-command-says-unknown-command",b:"3> Cause. The derived name follows the title. Retitle a bundle and its old command is gone after the next Reload; a new bundle has no command until a Reload. Fix. Reload, then check the name"},
{t:"Clicking does nothing",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#clicking-does-nothing",b:"3> Cause. A click sends _.PYNAME to the command line, like typing. With no drawing open there is nowhere to send it, and you get an Open a drawing first. toast. Fix. Open a drawing, then cli"},
{t:"(pyacad ...) returns nil",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#pyacad-returns-nil",b:"3> Cause. Either the script set no __result__ (or one json cannot serialise), or the run failed: the script raised, the target was not found, another script was running, or the bundle is out"},
{t:"The script does not do what you wrote",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-script-does-not-do-what-you-wrote",b:"2> Nothing happens at all # Cause. The work is in a function nobody calls. Defining main() and never calling it loads cleanly, raises nothing and opens no window. (It is not the __main__ gua"},
{t:"Nothing happens at all",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#nothing-happens-at-all",b:"3> Cause. The work is in a function nobody calls. Defining main() and never calling it loads cleanly, raises nothing and opens no window. (It is not the __main__ guard: both engines set __na"},
{t:"A window opens with almost nothing in it",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-window-opens-with-almost-nothing-in-it",b:"3> Cause. Something called print() . The output window opens on the first write, so a stray print , a debug line or a library warning is enough. Fix. Use pyacad.toast for one-line statuses; "},
{t:"A toast says the tool requires another AutoCAD",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-toast-says-the-tool-requires-another-autocad",b:"3> text Copy Cause. min_host_version or max_host_version in the bundle's bundle.yaml . The button still shows, by design, and refuses on click. A toast says something is still running # text"},
{t:"A toast says something is still running",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-toast-says-something-is-still-running",b:"3> text Copy Cause. One pyACAD script runs at a time, process-wide. A long script pumps the message loop to repaint, and the pump delivers your second click, which is refused rather than run"},
{t:"Errors",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#errors",b:"2> eLockViolation # Cause. Code wrote to the drawing without the document lock. A command has it for free, but a hook, startup.py , a dock pane's event handler, the console or a modeless win"},
{t:"eLockViolation",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#elockviolation",b:"3> Cause. Code wrote to the drawing without the document lock. A command has it for free, but a hook, startup.py , a dock pane's event handler, the console or a modeless window does not. Fix"},
{t:"RuntimeError: No drawing is open.",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#runtimeerror-no-drawing-is-open",b:"3> Cause. pyacad.doc.active() , and everything that reads the drawing, needs an active drawing. A ribbon click never starts a script without one, but code outside a command can run with none"},
{t:"ImportError: could not load the AutoCAD .NET API",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#importerror-could-not-load-the-autocad-net-api",b:"3> text Copy Cause. An API function of a pyacad module was called outside AutoCAD: in a unit test, or a script run by a plain Python. Fix. Inside AutoCAD nothing is wrong. Outside it, test t"},
{t:"Unknown engine",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#unknown-engine",b:"3> text Copy Cause. A typo in engine: in bundle.yaml or extension.yaml . The only values are ironpython and cpython , and the check happens at click time, so the button looks normal until it"},
{t:"No CPython found",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#no-cpython-found",b:"3> text Copy Cause. A bundle asks for engine: cpython and pyACAD found no usable interpreter: none in config.json , none in PYACAD_CPYTHON , and no 64-bit Python 3.7 to 3.13 in the registry."},
{t:"import pyacad fails in every script",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#import-pyacad-fails-in-every-script",b:"3> Cause. The runtime could not find the library folder. Logged once, at boot. text Copy Fix. Clear a wrong \"pyacadlib\" key in config.json (a missing folder there is also logged), or reinsta"},
{t:"import os fails on IronPython",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#import-os-fails-on-ironpython",b:"3> Cause. IronPython uses the standard library shipped in the runtime folder. If that folder is missing, every standard-library import fails while pyacad itself may still load. CPython bundl"},
{t:"A traceback appeared in the output window",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-traceback-appeared-in-the-output-window",b:"3> Cause. Your script raised. The engine formats the traceback into the output window and the log on purpose, because there is no console to send it to. Fix. Read the frames from the bottom "},
{t:"Hooks and startup.py",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#hooks-and-startup-py",b:"2> A hook failed three times and went quiet # text Copy Cause. A hook that fails three times in a row is disabled for the session, so a broken hook cannot fire a traceback on every selection"},
{t:"A hook failed three times and went quiet",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-hook-failed-three-times-and-went-quiet",b:"3> text Copy Cause. A hook that fails three times in a row is disabled for the session, so a broken hook cannot fire a traceback on every selection change. A success in between resets the co"},
{t:"A hook does not fire",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-hook-does-not-fire",b:"3> The file name must be one of the 20 event names exactly, such as doc-saved.py . Anything else is logged and skipped: Hook '...': unknown event 'on-save' - skipped. Valid names: ... A new "},
{t:"startup.py failed",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#startup-py-failed",b:"3> text Copy Cause and fix. The traceback is in the log after startup.py failed for 'Acme' . The extension's buttons load regardless. Fix the script and Reload, which runs every startup.py a"},
{t:"A dock pane does not appear",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-dock-pane-does-not-appear",b:"2> Some dock panes could not be built , with the pane named: its pane.xaml did not parse or its script.py raised. The log line starts Dock panes with problems: and the traceback follows. No "},
{t:"A keyboard shortcut does not work",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#a-keyboard-shortcut-does-not-work",b:"2> A chord fires only on a fresh key press (not auto-repeat), only while the AutoCAD main window is in front, and never while focus is in a text box, the command line included, so it never s"},
{t:"My edits do not take effect",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#my-edits-do-not-take-effect",b:"2> Three loops, at very different costs. Knowing which one an edit falls into saves more time than any other fact in this guide. Headless runs # pyacad run writes everything to its run folde"},
{t:"Headless runs",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#headless-runs",b:"2> pyacad run writes everything to its run folder, %APPDATA%\\pyACAD\\runs\\<yyyyMMdd-HHmmss> : See pyacad.exe and headless runs . Other tools for finding out # Alt+Click # Alt+Click any pyACAD"},
{t:"Other tools for finding out",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#other-tools-for-finding-out",b:"2> Alt+Click # Alt+Click any pyACAD button to open its bundle folder in Explorer with script.py selected. The fastest way from \"which file is this?\" to the file. The console # The Console bu"},
{t:"Alt+Click",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#alt-click",b:"3> Alt+Click any pyACAD button to open its bundle folder in Explorer with script.py selected. The fastest way from \"which file is this?\" to the file. The console # The Console button ( PYCON"},
{t:"The console",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#the-console",b:"3> The Console button ( PYCONSOLE ) opens an interactive IronPython prompt inside AutoCAD with the pyACAD library on the path: try an expression against the open drawing before you put it in"},
{t:"Logging from your own script",p:"Troubleshooting",u:"troubleshooting.html#logging-from-your-own-script",b:"3> python Copy Lines go to pyACAD.log , labelled with your tool's title (or the hook or startup.py they came from). Warnings and errors are also echoed into the output window, but only when "},
{t:"Style guide for tools",p:"Page",u:"style-guide.html",b:"The conventions every shipped tool follows, in code, in look and in behaviour, so that twenty tools written by ten people still feel like one product."},
{t:"Code conventions",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#code-conventions",b:"2> The bundle # One bundle, one job. A tool that does two things is two bundles, or one bundle with a config.py for its settings. Name folders the way the button reads. Layer_Mapper.pushbutt"},
{t:"The bundle",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#the-bundle",b:"3> One bundle, one job. A tool that does two things is two bundles, or one bundle with a config.py for its settings. Name folders the way the button reads. Layer_Mapper.pushbutton shows as \""},
{t:"The script",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#the-script",b:"3> Imports go at module level, straight after the docstring: from pyacad import with the modules in alphabetical order. Top-level code , no if __name__ == '__main__': wrapper. Helper functio"},
{t:"The look, in one paragraph",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#the-look-in-one-paragraph",b:"2> Neutral grays, hairlines and whitespace carry the structure. The Windows accent appears in exactly three places: the primary action, the selection rail and the focus ring. Numbers are qui"},
{t:"Accent in three places",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#accent-in-three-places",b:"2> .sg1-t { font: 11.5px 'Segoe UI Variable Text','Segoe UI',sans-serif; fill: var(--ink); } .sg1-m { font: 10.5px 'Segoe UI Variable Text','Segoe UI',sans-serif; fill: var(--muted); } .sg1-"},
{t:"The slop test",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#the-slop-test",b:"2> Before shipping any surface, check it against the tells below. If a screenshot of your tool could pass for a template demo, it fails. The goal is an interface a Windows user trusts at onc"},
{t:"Type",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#type",b:"2> The typeface is Segoe UI Variable, falling back to Segoe UI. Sentence case everywhere, never all caps, not even for section headers. Tabular figures wherever numbers line up. Layout # Col"},
{t:"Layout",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#layout",b:"2> Colour # Twelve tokens plus the accent. If a colour you want is not in this table, the answer is one of these tokens, not a new colour. Token Light Dark Used for Paper #FFFFFF #202020 Win"},
{t:"Colour",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#colour",b:"2> Twelve tokens plus the accent. If a colour you want is not in this table, the answer is one of these tokens, not a new colour. Read the theme with script.is_dark_theme() , which follows A"},
{t:"How a tool has to behave",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#how-a-tool-has-to-behave",b:"2> The visual rules keep tools looking alike. These keep them trustworthy, which is the harder half. Each one has a failure mode a real tool has shipped with. Report every outcome # A tool t"},
{t:"Report every outcome",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#report-every-outcome",b:"3> A tool that does its job and shows nothing is indistinguishable from a tool that crashed silently. Every path out of your script ends in something the user can see, with the single except"},
{t:"Cancelling is silent",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#cancelling-is-silent",b:"3> The exception. When the user cancels a dialog or presses Esc at a prompt, say nothing at all. They know what they did, and \"Cancelled\" is a notification about their own hand. Do Don't Fai"},
{t:"Failure is a sentence, not a traceback",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#failure-is-a-sentence-not-a-traceback",b:"3> Predictable failures (a missing file, a read-only drawing, a value the user typed wrong) get caught and reported in plain words that say the fix. Genuine bugs should still raise: the runt"},
{t:"One undo step per user action",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#one-undo-step-per-user-action",b:"3> A tool started as a command is one undo group: AutoCAD opens it before your first line and closes it after your last, so a single U takes back everything, however many transactions the sc"},
{t:"Counted progress, and a way out",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#counted-progress-and-a-way-out",b:"3> Show progress for anything that can run longer than a second or two, and offer Cancel for anything that can run longer than several seconds. Put the count in the label: \"Checking 4,200 of"},
{t:"Empty states are states",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#empty-states-are-states",b:"3> Nothing selected, no results, no drawing open, no xrefs to manage: each of these is a real answer and needs saying, along with what the user should do about it. Do Don't Never open a wind"},
{t:"Never open a window to say one line",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#never-open-a-window-to-say-one-line",b:"3> A bare print() opens the whole output window to show one sentence, and if that is all the script writes, the user gets a nearly empty window. It reads as a bug because it looks exactly li"},
{t:"Speak the drawing's language",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#speak-the-drawing-s-language",b:"3> The user knows what they see in AutoCAD: layers, blocks, attributes, xrefs, layouts, model space. They do not know your variable names, and they should never meet an API type name. Do Don"},
{t:"Copy rules",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#copy-rules",b:"2> Sentence case. \"Merge layers\", not \"Merge Layers\". (Button titles are the one place the shipped tools use title case: \"Blocks to Excel\".) No em dashes or en dashes. A comma, a colon, a se"},
{t:"The same result, twice",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#the-same-result-twice",b:"2> .sg2-t { font: 11.5px 'Segoe UI Variable Text','Segoe UI',sans-serif; fill: var(--ink); } .sg2-m { font: 10.5px 'Segoe UI Variable Text','Segoe UI',sans-serif; fill: var(--muted); } .sg2-"},
{t:"Before you ship",p:"Style guide for tools",u:"style-guide.html#before-you-ship",b:"2> Run your tool against this list. It takes two minutes and catches almost everything. The docstring or tooltip: says what the tool does in a full sentence. Anything that calls the tool by "}
